      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Baker",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Spanish explorer Gonzalo Lopez de Haro called the volcano La Gran Montana del Carmel in 1790 before it became Mount Baker.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/23225",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Mount Baker is the youngest volcano in the North Cascades, probably no older than 80,000 years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Baker",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Its crater is the second‑most thermally active in the Cascade Range, trailing only Mount St. Helens.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Baker",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mount Baker’s glaciers hold more ice than all other Cascade volcanoes combined, except Mount Rainier.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
